1
00:00:00,950 --> 00:00:03,450
This magnet is one watt of heat.

2
00:00:03,550 --> 00:00:07,030
It appeared a moment ago, when a server answered a question.

3
00:00:07,150 --> 00:00:10,350
Almost every watt a server uses turns into heat.

4
00:00:10,390 --> 00:00:14,050
A single AI rack can give off more than 100 kilowatts.

5
00:00:14,550 --> 00:00:16,580
So, where does the heat go?

6
00:00:17,950 --> 00:00:21,450
First, air carries it off the chips, into a cooling unit.

7
00:00:21,750 --> 00:00:25,450
There, it warms a loop of chilled water, which flows to a chiller.

8
00:00:25,950 --> 00:00:27,940
The chiller hands the heat to a second loop,

9
00:00:27,980 --> 00:00:29,880
which climbs up to the roof.

10
00:00:30,350 --> 00:00:32,800
On cold days, the chiller can even switch off,

11
00:00:32,840 --> 00:00:35,120
and the roof does the job alone.

12
00:00:35,550 --> 00:00:37,820
Up here is the cooling tower.

13
00:00:37,950 --> 00:00:40,630
Warm water is sprayed over a honeycomb called fill,

14
00:00:40,670 --> 00:00:43,770
while a big fan pulls outside air up through it.

15
00:00:43,950 --> 00:00:48,880
A little of the water evaporates, and evaporating water carries a lot of heat away.

16
00:00:49,350 --> 00:00:53,870
Lose about 1 percent, and the rest comes out around 6 degrees cooler.

17
00:00:54,350 --> 00:00:59,900
It's the same trick as sweating, and it uses far less electricity than cooling with air alone.

18
00:01:00,350 --> 00:01:02,310
And our one watt of heat?

19
00:01:03,150 --> 00:01:04,950
Gone. Into the sky.

20
00:01:05,950 --> 00:01:08,610
What it leaves behind is a water bill.

21
00:01:08,750 --> 00:01:11,990
A big site can evaporate millions of litres a day.

22
00:01:12,150 --> 00:01:15,480
So operators measure the litres used for every kilowatt hour,

23
00:01:15,520 --> 00:01:18,500
and turn to recycled water where they can.

24
00:01:23,950 --> 00:01:26,330
From chip, to water, to sky.
